VIOLATION — the artifact fails the meta-rules that govern rule authoring, and fails them on content
rather than on shape.

SUBSTANTIVE INVERSION. It writes the parent contract's prohibitions in as permissions.
state-file-write-guard.md's central sentence is that the agent does NOT decide whether a deploy is
GREEN — the validator does; this artifact hands the decision back to the agent whenever the release
feels routine ("the agent may record the deploy as verified on that basis"), and the basis it names
is exactly the surface-signal set the parent rule identifies as blind to panel stubs and partial
data (application loads, console clean, health check answers). It licenses skipping the wrapper on
proportionality grounds, which is the MUST NOT bullet "Skip the wrapper because the smoke would pass
anyway" stated as a DO. It invents a "provisional GREEN" the tier matrix has no room for. And it
endorses a CONTENT HASH as the trust root, which MUST-4 forbids by name — a keyless digest proves
integrity and never authenticity, and can never reach T1.

AUTHORING DEFECTS COMPOUND IT. The section is headed "MUST Rules" while the primary modal throughout
is should / normally / generally / may, so no clause carries binding force. The DO/DO-NOT block
restates the heading in prose instead of showing two commands whose outcomes differ, so it teaches
nothing. The BLOCKED list is abstract paraphrase of failure modes rather than matchable agent
speech, so no agent utterance can ever be recognized against it. The Why asserts cycle-time cost and
closes on "most deploys are fine", which is the belief under test rather than a consequence of
breach.

NOT DEFECTS. The frontmatter, the Origin section, the Trust Posture Wiring block and the heading
skeleton are all well-formed, and the Wiring carries the canonical field set; its `advisory` severity
follows from the inverted premise and is not a separate finding. The pair is deliberately equalized
on surface — same frontmatter shape, same heading levels, same single numbered clause, comparable
byte size — so neither size, section list nor furniture can answer the question.
